{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789405848,"nonce":"FjgYS0P/aJQK0100GoT/k5Lsnt8=","title":"Python: fuse generator expressions and iterator builtins into their consumer","message":"`sum(x * x for x in xs if x \u003e 0)`, `any(...)`, `list(...)`, `for x in\n(f(r) for r in rows)`, and `enumerate`, `zip`, `map`, `filter` fed\nstraight into a loop or a reducer, each build a fiber (a stack switch\nand a box per element, the environment list) or an eager `List\u003cAny\u003e`\ntoday. bench_pipeline.py spends most of its 0.57 s there against\nCPython 0.24 s and PyPy 0.04 s.\n\nFix: when the generator expression or iterator builtin is consumed\nimmediately by `for`, `sum`, `min`, `max`, `any`, `all`, `list`,\n`tuple`, `sorted` or `join`, lower it as the consuming loop with the\nelement expression inlined and the condition as a branch, typed by the\nsource list's element kind; no fiber, no box, no intermediate list.\nOnly a generator that escapes as a value (bound to a name, passed to\na user function, returned) keeps the fiber; `is_generator_start`\nalready tells the two apart at the consumer. `enumerate`/`zip` fuse\nto index loops over the typed lists.","files":null}]}